3A. Curse-of-Knowledge Removal Protocol

The first pass still risks beginning with human categories—god, worship, morality, death—and dressing them in another animal’s senses. A harder method begins before category and before language.

Descent sequence

```text
1. Suspend human nouns.
2. Remove knowledge unavailable to the creature.
3. Remove senses and actions unavailable to its body.
4. Set its experienced time, distance, memory, hunger, vulnerability, and social dependence.
5. Generate a stream of salience: near/far, safe/dangerous, kin/other, known/changed, approach/avoid.
6. Identify recurrences before naming them.
7. Allow expectation, attachment, avoidance, and coordinated practice to form.
8. Only then translate the resulting structure into human language.
9. Record what the translation added, distorted, or made falsely discrete.
```

The target is not perfect access to another creature’s subjectivity; that is unavailable. The target is disciplined de-centering: progressively subtracting what the human interpreter knows but the modeled creature could not.

Preconceptual state vector

```yaml
now:
  bodily_need: [hunger, thirst, heat, pain, mating, rest]
  sensed_change: [scent, vibration, pressure, light, sound, field, touch]
  valence: [drawn, repelled, uncertain]
  agency_guess: [self-caused, other-caused, ambient, unknown]
  proximity: [inside-body, reachable, near, beyond-action]
  social_relation: [offspring, parent, ally, rival, stranger, predator, prey]
  recurrence: [novel, familiar, seasonal, ancestral]
  control: [controllable, influenceable, escapable, inescapable]
```

Sacred structure is permitted to emerge only after repeated states form a durable pattern.

3B. Inside Renderings: Before Human Translation

The following passages deliberately avoid claiming literal animal speech. They are first-person-shaped reconstructions of attention and consequence.

Elephant: the remembered path

Humans absent

Heat. Dryness. The young one slows. The ground carries weight from far away. An older body turns before water can be smelled. Others turn with her. The route is not visible, but her movement reduces uncertainty. Later, a place holds bone and familiar odor without familiar motion. The herd slows there. Touch finds what presence left behind.

What humans might later call sacred is not initially “ancestor worship.” It is the lived fact that memory inside one body can keep many bodies alive, and that a missing individual can remain present in paths, pauses, and changed behavior.

Possible sects:

• Matriarchal Path Keepers: old route-memory is highest authority.
• Young-Rain Revisionists: younger herds follow newly reliable water created by climate change.
• Border Herds: repeated conflict makes vigilance and concealment sacred.
• Abundance Herds: stable water shifts emphasis from route fidelity toward social continuity and play.

Humans present

A distant odor predicts thunder without clouds. A path becomes absence. A body falls without tooth, claw, or warning. Elsewhere, a strange upright animal approaches and water appears; wounds stop hurting; boundaries exclude lions and also block old routes.

The human does not enter as one coherent category. Different elephant populations could inherit incompatible human cosmologies:

• The Far-Striking Ones: humans as causation without proximity—death detached from visible force.
• The Water-Bringers: humans as landscape modifiers associated with relief.
• The Fence-Makers: humans as invisible territorial law made physical.
• The Two-Handed Ambivalence: the same form predicts rescue in one lineage and annihilation in another.

The delta is not merely “humans become gods.” Human presence fractures causal learning because one body-form produces radically different consequences.

Wolf: the moving boundary

Humans absent

The world arrives layered in scent-time. This mark is fresh; that one is old. Kin are not an idea but a moving field of familiar bodies whose positions alter what can be attempted. Hunger pulls outward. Injury pulls inward. Large prey can become food only when several bodies converge on the same opening.

The sacred structure is coordinated relation under risk. Rank is not simply privilege; it changes who initiates, who yields, who eats, who mates, and who absorbs danger.

Possible sects:

• Central-Line wolves: experienced breeders experience order as responsibility and reproductive continuity.
• Peripheral wolves: low-rank members experience the same order as constrained access and conditional belonging.
• Dispersers: solitary travel makes boundary-crossing, ambiguity, and self-reliance central.
• Prey-Rich packs: coordination confirms abundance.
• Famine packs: failed hunts create schism over leadership, route, or target choice.

Humans present

One human arrangement leaves carcasses. Another kills wolves that approach them. Livestock are unusually vulnerable prey surrounded by disproportionate retaliation. Roads fracture territory; gunfire acts across distances no wolf action can answer.

Possible human-conditioned sects:

• Ghost Walkers: avoid human scent, light, and open ground; survival validates withdrawal.
• Edge Takers: exploit farms, refuse the old prey law, accept catastrophic variance.
• Road Readers: treat machine paths as recurring lethal currents with learnable timing.
• Habituated Lineages: humans become background rather than absolute taboo—until a regime change breaks the inherited model.

Here, sect formation follows different reinforcement histories, not doctrinal argument.

Crow: the face that persists

Humans absent

Faces remain after encounters. A dangerous face seen by one can alter the calls and movements of many. Objects are not fixed in meaning: a hard thing becomes access; a height becomes safety; another animal’s attention becomes a manipulable variable. The unseen future can be changed by hiding something now.

The sacred center is persistent social consequence: memory travels between individuals, and cleverness converts environmental structure into opportunity.

Possible sects:

• Sentinels: danger knowledge and alarm credibility organize value.
• Cache Keepers: concealment, memory, and counter-theft dominate.
• Juvenile Innovators: play and object manipulation produce new practices resisted by conservative adults.
• Mobbing Coalitions: collective confrontation is validated where solitary flight fails.

Humans present

The upright faces differ individually. One face feeds. One pursues. Some machines crack food; others kill. Human refuse generates abundance disconnected from natural seasons. Cities produce warm islands, vertical refuges, transparent traps, and scheduled rivers of traffic.

Possible sects:

• Gift Lineages: reciprocal attention toward particular humans becomes stable practice.
• Refuse Priests: mastery of human waste cycles displaces older seasonal knowledge.
• Machine Oracles: traffic rhythm becomes a usable environmental regularity.
• Old-Wood Traditionalists: urban novelty remains suspect; inherited nonhuman foraging retains authority.

Crow cosmology becomes more individuated around humans because crows can distinguish particular faces: “human” separates into remembered persons rather than remaining one species-level force.

Whale: the broken ocean

Humans absent

Near darkness. Pressure. The body hears relation farther than it sees form. A call leaves and the ocean gives it shape. Other voices occupy distances that vision cannot hold. Routes persist across lifetimes through social transmission. Silence may mean distance, death, obstruction, or listening.

The sacred center is continuity through sound. Knowledge is not light falling upon a thing; it is contact made through emission, reception, and remembered song.

Possible sects:

• Route Singers: inherited migration and feeding grounds define continuity.
• Dialect Clans: group identity resides in patterned sound.
• Deep Divers: vertical passage and pressure transitions structure the experienced cosmos.
• Mothers and Calves: proximity, breathing rhythm, and teaching narrow the sacred unit to the vulnerable pair.

Humans present

The ocean acquires ceaseless low-frequency machinery. Sound no longer reliably indicates life or geography. Sudden noise overwhelms orientation. Vast moving walls cross routes. Ropes act like invisible predation that does not eat.

Possible sects:

• Quiet-Water Conservators: retreat validates distance from shipping corridors.
• Noise-Habituated Navigators: altered calling and timing permit survival near traffic.
• Route Loyalists: inherited passage continues despite new mortality.
• Route Breakers: deviation becomes the new continuity.

This creates a profound theological delta: the ancestral signal may remain faithful while the environment that once made it true has changed.

Octopus: the temporary center

Humans absent

The edge of self is active. One arm enters where the eye cannot. Texture becomes decision before the whole body resolves it. Skin answers the world by changing. A hard boundary may contain an opening detectable only through touch. Safety comes from becoming locally continuous with surroundings; food comes from revealing discontinuity in another creature’s concealment.

The sacred center is not a sovereign self but adaptive coherence among partially independent explorations.

Possible sects:

• Den Keepers: place memory, arrangement, and defensible return stabilize existence.
• Open-Water Wanderers: motion and rapid transformation outrank place.
• Mimics: identity is strategic plurality; fixed appearance becomes error.
• Brooding Mothers: the entire remaining future narrows into guarding eggs, ending in death.

Humans present

Some artificial objects become shelter. Others confine invisibly until touched. A human hand may offer stimulation, food, capture, or pain. Human-built environments reward curiosity and punish it unpredictably.

Possible sects:

• Artifact Users: artificial forms are incorporated into den and tool ecology.
• Glass-Bound Experimentalists: repeated invisible barriers create a world where sight and reach disagree.
• Contact Avoiders: novel chemical and visual signatures predict capture.
• Human Interactors: particular repeated humans become complex environmental partners.

The crucial delta is an interface contradiction: visible space is not necessarily traversable space.

Bee: the warm many

Humans absent

No single bee possesses the hive’s entire condition. Temperature, scent, contact, dance, food flow, and brood state are encountered locally. Many small acts stabilize a warm reproductive center that outlives workers. A dance does not command; it changes the probability of attention and recruitment.

The sacred center is the continuing superordinate pattern, not the replaceable worker.

Possible sects are closer to role-conditioned cosmologies than explicit factions:

• Nurses: reality centers on brood, temperature, and immediate care.
• Foragers: sun, distance, risk, novelty, and return dominate.
• Guards: foreign scent makes boundary purity central.
• Scouts: uncertain possibilities matter more than established flows.
• Swarm factions: competing nest sites accumulate support until one route reaches behavioral quorum.

Humans present

Enclosures open. Smoke alters alarm and feeding behavior. Food abundance appears in unnaturally uniform fields and vanishes at once. The hive may be moved while the local sky remains unchanged. Chemical injury arrives without predator scent.

Possible sect-like adaptations:

• Apiary-Stable colonies: disturbance predicts survival, space, and managed food.
• Feral Avoiders: enclosed cavities associated with capture are rejected.
• Monocrop Opportunists: exploit synchronized abundance while becoming vulnerable to sudden absence.
• Chemical Survivors: inherited behavior and biology shift under pressures no individual can represent.

For the bee, “sect” often exists through distributed preference and lineage selection, not competing narrated beliefs.

3C. Human-Presence Counterfactual

Humans alter animal cosmogenesis through several distinct operators:

|Human effect            |Experienced form to animal                   |Possible sacred/cosmological consequence                        |
|------------------------|---------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------|
|Remote killing          |consequence without reachable cause          |invisible agency, radical avoidance, or learned helplessness    |
|Feeding                 |abundance detached from native effort/season |patron relation, dependency, schedule ritual, risk normalization|
|Habitat engineering     |geography changes faster than inherited model|traditionalist/revisionist split                                |
|Captivity               |visible affordance blocked by invisible rule |interface distrust; keeper-centered causality                   |
|Protection              |predators or scarcity removed by boundary    |sanctuary model; weakened calibration beyond boundary           |
|Individual recognition  |particular humans predict particular outcomes|personalized rather than species-level cosmology                |
|Noise/light pollution   |ancestral signal loses reliability           |revelation crisis; signal adaptation; route schism              |
|Domestication           |human intention enters reproductive ecology  |attachment, neoteny, cross-species authority, hybrid social unit|
|Waste and infrastructure|new niches appear                            |opportunist sects, inherited skill displacement                 |

Humans are unusual not because animals must experience us as divine, but because we repeatedly create causal asymmetry: effects arrive beyond the animal’s ability to reciprocate, inspect, or control.

3D. Sectogenesis Tensor

Intraspecies cosmologies diverge when members of the same species repeatedly occupy different coordinates:

```text
Sect = f(
  trophic_position,
  predator_pressure,
  dominance_rank,
  age,
  sex_and_reproductive_role,
  kinship,
  task_role,
  habitat,
  resource_regime,
  mobility,
  trauma_history,
  human_exposure,
  inherited_practice
)
```

Food-chain position

• Apex experiences: the world more often yields to approach; threat is exceptional, territorial, or conspecific.
• Mesopredator experiences: the same body alternates between hunter and hunted; cosmology is conditional and double-sided.
• Prey experiences: open space, stillness, concealment, group vigilance, and timing become existential operators.
• Scavenger experiences: death is also opportunity; following danger can produce nourishment after the danger passes.
• Parasite experiences: another body is simultaneously habitat, resource, boundary, and world.

These are not merely different risk levels. They create different primitives. To prey, concealment may be preservation; to a predator, concealment may be approach. The same darkness can therefore be sanctuary, weapon, or uncertainty.

Internal position

• A dominant member may experience hierarchy as stable coordination; a subordinate may experience the same hierarchy as unpredictable access.
• A parent experiences threat through vulnerable offspring; a solitary nonparent experiences it through the body alone.
• A juvenile meets novelty before inherited categories harden; an elder carries longer causal memory but may be less able to act.
• A scout receives reward for uncertainty; a guard receives reward for suspicion.
• A migrant experiences home as a recurring route; a territorial individual experiences home as defended continuity of place.

Thus “one species, one religion” is structurally implausible even inside the thought experiment. Shared embodiment supplies a family resemblance, while role and history generate sects.

3E. Two-Axis Delta Experiment

To separate body from environment, run four cells:

|                  |Humans absent            |Humans present                        |
|------------------|-------------------------|--------------------------------------|
|Species A / role 1|baseline Umwelt cosmology|human-conditioned delta               |
|Species A / role 2|internal-status delta    |interaction of status × human presence|

Then repeat with Species B in the same habitat.

```text
Δ_body        = Species A − Species B, environment held approximately constant
Δ_human       = humans present − humans absent, species/role held constant
Δ_status      = role 1 − role 2, species/environment held constant
Δ_interaction = combined change − sum of isolated changes
```

The interaction term matters most. A human is not the same event to an elephant matriarch, a calf, a crop-raiding bull, and a poaching survivor. Nor is “predator” the same ontology for prey, rival predators, dependent young, and scavengers waiting in its wake.

